## Why This Is Asked

This question probes memory reclamation under contention, cross-service isolation, and CAS-based synchronization in a NUMA-aware kernel, testing both design and practicality.

## Key Concepts

- Per-service quotas in a shared cache
- Deterministic eviction scheduling
- CAS-based victim claims
- Backpressure signaling to avoid thrash
- NUMA-aware replenishment and cross-slab coordination

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo sketch: CAS-based page claim within a slab
typedef struct { int claimed; } page_t;
typedef struct { page_t *pages; size_t n; } slab_t;
int evict_one(slab_t *s) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < s->n; i++) {
    if (__sync_bool_compare_and_swap(&s->pages[i].claimed, 0, 1)) {
      // evict this page
      return 1;
    }
  }
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this policy as slabs resize dynamically under changing workloads?
- What tests would prove tail-latency guarantees across bursty traffic and multi-tenant workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about memory cgroup enforcement, scalable reclaim under mixed workloads, and kernel-level resource management.

## Key Concepts
- Per-container quotas and deficit calculation
- Per-page state tracking (active, inactive, referenced)
- NUMA-aware page replacement decisions
- Interaction with swap and lazy reclaim mechanisms
- Concurrency-safe quota accounting
- Starvation prevention and thrashing avoidance

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal C-like skeleton of reclaim loop
for (each node in nodes) {
    while (deficit[node] > 0) {
        // Select overcommitted cgroup as victim
        struct cgroup *victim = pick_victim_cgroup(node);

        // Evict pages from victim (prefer inactive, anon > file)
        struct page *page = lru_scan(victim, node);
        if (page) {
            if (try_to_reclaim(page))
                deficit[node] -= PAGE_SIZE;
        }
    }
}
```

